                    warn_coherence_range:   `Coherence must be a percentage between 0 and 100.`,

                    coach_default:          `Decision logged.`,
                    coach_r1_economics:     `<strong>That's the right diagnosis.</strong> A 1.2x LTV/CAC means every acquired user loses money — and growth at those economics burns the runway faster, it doesn't save it. Fix the economics before you scale, not after.`,
                    coach_r1_marketing:     `<strong>More marketing on a 1.2x model just spends faster to lose more.</strong> You'd triple users at a CAC of €34 against an LTV of €41 — that sinks a 9-month runway. Marketing doesn't fix unit economics; the model does.`,
                    coach_r1_product:       `<strong>Features may lift retention, but you're betting the runway on it.</strong> Even generous retention barely closes a 1.2x-to-3.0x gap on the consumer model. The structural problem is who pays and for what — not the feature list.`,
                    coach_r1_runway:        `<strong>'We're just early' is what every venture says before it runs out of cash.</strong> The €6M closes the gap for two years, but at 1.2x you'll burn it and arrive at the same cliff. Investors fund a fixed model, not a postponed one.`,
                    coach_r1_sharp:         `<strong>Sharp diagnosis.</strong> You named the broken economics AND the unpriced sustainable value cities will pay for. That's the thread: stop giving emissions cuts and city outcomes away free, price them, and the 1.2x problem becomes a 3.0x opportunity.`,
                    coach_r1_greenpremium:  `<strong>A consumer green premium is a small lever on the wrong customer.</strong> Citizens won't pay much for eco-routing, and it keeps you in the broken consumer model. The side that pays for outcomes is the city, not the user.`,
                    coach_r1_brand:         `<strong>Treating sustainability as a brand halo is Common Error number two.</strong> You're creating verified emissions cuts and city value, then giving it away while paying to acquire consumers. Where in the revenue model does that value become money?`,
                    coach_r1_none:          `<strong>'We capture all the value we create' is exactly the blind spot.</strong> You cut city emissions and improve mobility outcomes and charge nobody for it. The unpriced sustainable value is the revenue your model is leaving on the table.`,
                    coach_r1_lowtarget:     `<strong>Committing to barely above 1.2x tells investors you accept a broken model.</strong> The benchmark is 3.0x for a reason — it's the line between a venture that scales and one that burns. Aim for the mechanism that gets you there.`,
                    coach_r1_hightarget:    `<strong>Promising 5.0x with no mechanism signals you don't understand the number.</strong> Investors will ask exactly how pricing, retention and CAC produce it. Commit to a defensible ~3.0x you can actually evidence.`,
                    coach_r2_consumer:      `<strong>The consumer app is the big-TAM, weak-economics, weak-moat trap.</strong> Pouring the €6M into acquisition on a 1.2x model accelerates the burn and builds no moat against the super-app. You're spending faster to lose more.`,
                    coach_r2_saas:          `<strong>B2B city SaaS/data is the defensible economics play.</strong> Cities pay for verified emissions cuts and mobility insights — strong margins, sticky retention, and sustainability becomes priced revenue. Slower sales, but the unit economics actually reach 3.0x.`,
                    coach_r2_platform:      `<strong>A platform has real network effects — but you have one pilot city.</strong> Others build on you only once both sides and scale exist. Fund it properly and find the wedge, or it stays a sub-scale promise the super-app overtakes.`,
                    coach_r2_everything:    `<strong>You're pitching a consumer app, a city-SaaS and a platform at once — that's Common Error number four.</strong> Investors hear a startup that doesn't know what it is. With €6M and 24 months you build one of these well. Choose, and say why now.`,
                    coach_r2_ads:           `<strong>Ad-funding keeps sustainability as a marketing line, not revenue.</strong> Attention monetisation on a mobility app is thin and leaves the 1.2x economics untouched. The value cities will pay for goes unpriced again.`,
                    coach_r2_unchanged:     `<strong>Leaving pricing unchanged is deferring the only decision that matters.</strong> 'Revisit monetisation after the raise' is exactly the postponed model investors won't fund. The revenue mechanic is the redesign.`,
                    coach_r2_unfocused:     `<strong>Spreading the capital thin reaches scale in nothing.</strong> A model needs concentrated investment to build the wedge and the moat. Pick the model and back it — half-funding it half-funds the outcome.`,
                    coach_r3_ops:           `<strong>Operations is the right instinct — AI on a task cities pay for.</strong> Automating municipal emissions reporting lifts margin and wins B2B deals. Make sure it sits under the model that actually sells to cities, or the fit is wasted.`,
                    coach_r3_ops_fit:       `<strong>That's AI as a value driver, not a slogan.</strong> GenAI automating municipal reporting, under a B2B city-SaaS model, turns a cost cities bear into a product they buy — it earns its €45K and strengthens exactly the model you chose.`,
                    coach_r3_consumer:      `<strong>Consumer trip-planning boosts engagement you can't monetise.</strong> It's the most visible AI and the least bankable — €45K/month of inference for nudges that don't reach 3.0x. Which use-case do customers actually pay for?`,
                    coach_r3_everywhere:    `<strong>You put an LLM in the app, the dashboard and the help desk — €45K a month, a fifth of your burn, for engagement you can't monetise.</strong> That's Common Error number three. Which single use-case do customers pay for? Cut the rest.`,
                    coach_r3_notnow:        `<strong>Deferring AI protects the runway — but forgoes the one use-case cities pay for.</strong> Discipline is right; total abstinence leaves margin and a B2B wedge on the table. The disciplined answer is one paid use-case, not zero.`,
                    coach_r3_overspend:     `<strong>You're spending near the full €45K with no use-case that monetises it.</strong> That's 20% of burn leaking on AI for show. Concentrate the spend on the operations task cities pay for, or pull it back.`,
                    coach_r3_inhouse:       `<strong>Building your own model now burns scarce runway for control you don't yet need.</strong> Validate the use-case on a foundation-model API first; fine-tune on your municipal data once ROI is proven. Don't pre-commit the cash to infrastructure.`,
                    coach_r4_data:          `<strong>Data partner to cities is a defensible position — but anchor it to a model that sells to cities.</strong> Own the municipal relationship and the outcome reporting and the super-app can't simply take it. Make sure the rest of the pitch points the same way.`,
                    coach_r4_data_fit:      `<strong>That's the coherent wedge.</strong> Preferred data/insights partner, under a B2B city-SaaS model, with AI automating the reporting — the super-app can court the city, but it doesn't own the verified-outcome relationship you do. That's a moat.`,
                    coach_r4_hub:           `<strong>Integration hub has strong network effects — if you reach both sides.</strong> Being the layer operators connect through is defensible, but it needs the platform model and scale behind it, not a single pilot.`,
                    coach_r4_hub_fit:       `<strong>Hub fits the platform play.</strong> If transit and charging operators build on you, the network effect compounds — but win the wedge city first so there's a reason for the second operator to join.`,
                    coach_r4_aggregator:    `<strong>Going consumer-aggregator is fighting the super-app on its own turf.</strong> It has more capital and a bigger app; head-on, it disintermediates you. The defensible ground is the city relationship and the verified outcomes, not the home screen.`,
                    coach_r4_discount:      `<strong>Undercutting on price and out-spending on acquisition is a war you lose.</strong> The super-app has deeper pockets — a price war just burns your 9-month runway faster. Compete on the defensible wedge, not the cheapest fare.`,
                    coach_r4_wait:          `<strong>Staying broad and waiting hands the city to the super-app.</strong> A platform needs the wedge before the giant signs the flagship municipality. Two quarters is the window; 'keep options open' spends it.`,
                    coach_r4_coherent:      `<strong>That's one story.</strong> The model, the AI plan and the ecosystem position all point the same way — B2B city value, AI on the reporting cities pay for, and the data-partner position. An investor pulls the thread and it holds. That is fundable.`,
                    coach_r4_falsecoherence: `<strong>You say it's one story, but the pieces don't fit.</strong> The model, the AI use-case and the position point in different directions — an investor pulls the first thread and it unravels. Coherence is the pieces aligning, not asserting they do.`,
                    coach_r4_incoherent:    `<strong>A model that says one thing, an AI plan another and a position a third is the incoherent raise.</strong> Investors fund a startup that knows what it is. Pull the three decisions into a single, defensible thread before you pitch.`,

                    sum_default:            `Decision made.`,
                    sum_r1_good:            `Named the broken economics and the unpriced city value.`,
